clg_data_analysis.md

# ── Generated artifacts ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every output here is reproducible from the Quickstart pipeline in ~6 minutes,
# and artifacts/models alone is 38 MB. Keep the folder skeleton, not the outputs.
artifacts/models/*
artifacts/logs/*
artifacts/diagnostics/*
artifacts/plots/*
artifacts/report/*
!artifacts/models/.gitkeep
!artifacts/logs/.gitkeep
!artifacts/diagnostics/.gitkeep
!artifacts/plots/.gitkeep
!artifacts/report/.gitkeep
# The per-slice returns behind the headline paired t-test. Small, and the whole
# point is that the statistic can be re-derived from committed data rather than
# quoted as a conclusion -- so this one output is tracked. The plots and REPORT.md
# stay ignored; they are regenerated by `generate_report`.
!artifacts/report/held_out_slices.json

# ── Campus GPU telemetry (Clg_Data/) ─────────────────────────────────────
# The 26 raw week-wise Grafana exports (1.1 MB) stay local: they are bulky,
# noisy, and only ever consumed by finalize_clg_dataset.py.
#
# Tracked instead: the analysis scripts, plus the two derived CSVs
# (gpu_telemetry_identified.csv, physics_validation_results.csv). The derived
# pair is what validate_physics_against_real_gpu.py actually reads and what
# configs/hardware_profiles.py cites as provenance, so a fresh clone can still
# reproduce the sim-to-real validation without the raw exports.
# Patterns are spelled out in full rather than as Clg_Data/GPU*.csv: on a
# case-insensitive filesystem that short form also swallows the derived
# gpu_telemetry_identified.csv, which must stay tracked.
Clg_Data/GPU Power Usage-*.csv
Clg_Data/GPU Temperature-*.csv
Clg_Data/GPU Utilization-*.csv
Clg_Data/GPU SM Clocks-*.csv
Clg_Data/merged_gpu_telemetry.csv
Clg_Data/*_per_series.csv

# ── Locally profiled GPU telemetry ───────────────────────────────────────
# Written by tools/gpu_profiler.py; hardware-specific, not reproducible.
data/local_gpu_trace.csv
